::ALG2_U01_L04_Q01::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q01<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which statement correctly defines the imaginary unit i? {
~ i = -1 #The imaginary unit is not equal to -1.
= i<sup>2</sup> = -1 #Correct. The imaginary unit is defined so that i squared equals -1.
~ i<sup>2</sup> = 1 #This would make i behave like 1 or -1, not an imaginary unit.
~ i = 0 #The imaginary unit is not zero.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q02::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q02<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is &radic;-25 simplified? {
~ -5 #Squaring -5 gives positive 25, not negative 25.
= 5i #Correct. &radic;-25 equals &radic;25 times i, which is 5i.
~ -25i #This uses the radicand instead of its square root and has the wrong magnitude.
~ 25i #The square root of 25 is 5, not 25.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q03::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q03<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is &radic;-12 simplified? {
~ 12i #This does not simplify the square root of 12.
~ -2&radic;3 #This is real and negative, not imaginary.
= 2i&radic;3 #Correct. &radic;-12 = i&radic;12 = 2i&radic;3.
~ 6i&radic;2 #This does not equal the simplified square root of 12.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q04::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q04<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is &radic;-50 simplified? {
~ 25i&radic;2 #This uses 25 instead of the square root factor 5.
= 5i&radic;2 #Correct. &radic;-50 = i&radic;50 = 5i&radic;2.
~ -5&radic;2 #This omits i, so it is not a square root of a negative number.
~ 10i&radic;5 #This does not simplify to &radic;50.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q05::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q05<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which number is the complex conjugate of 7 - 4i? {
~ -7 + 4i #This changes both the real part and the imaginary sign.
~ -7 - 4i #This changes the real part, which should stay the same.
= 7 + 4i #Correct. A complex conjugate keeps the real part and changes the sign of the imaginary part.
~ 4 - 7i #This switches the real and imaginary parts.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q06::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q06<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the discriminant of x<sup>2</sup> - 2x + 5 = 0? {
~ 24 #This adds 4ac instead of subtracting it.
= -16 #Correct. b squared minus 4ac is (-2) squared minus 4(1)(5), which is 4 - 20 = -16.
~ 16 #The magnitude is 16, but the sign should be negative.
~ -24 #This is not the result of b squared minus 4ac.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q07::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q07<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What is the discriminant of 2x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 7 = 0? {
~ 72 #This adds the terms instead of subtracting 4ac.
~ -12 #This does not multiply 4ac correctly.
= -40 #Correct. b squared minus 4ac is 16 - 56, which equals -40.
~ 40 #This has the correct magnitude but the wrong sign.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q08::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q08<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic equation has discriminant 0. What does this mean? {
~ It has two different real solutions. #Two different real solutions occur when the discriminant is positive.
= It has one repeated real solution. #Correct. A discriminant of zero makes the square-root part of the quadratic formula equal zero.
~ It has two complex non-real solutions. #Complex non-real solutions occur when the discriminant is negative.
~ It has no solution in any number system. #Quadratic equations still have solutions; the discriminant identifies their type.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q09::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q09<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic equation has discriminant 25. What type of solutions does it have? {
= Two real solutions #Correct. A positive discriminant gives two real solutions.
~ One repeated real solution #A repeated real solution occurs only when the discriminant is zero.
~ Two complex non-real solutions #Complex non-real solutions occur when the discriminant is negative.
~ No real or complex solutions #A quadratic equation has solutions in the complex number system.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q10::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q10<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>A quadratic equation has discriminant -9. What type of solutions does it have? {
~ Two real solutions #A positive discriminant gives two real solutions.
~ One repeated real solution #A discriminant of zero gives one repeated real solution.
= Two complex non-real solutions #Correct. A negative discriminant produces imaginary values in the quadratic formula.
~ No solutions in the complex number system #The complex number system includes solutions for this case.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q11::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q11<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> + 4 = 0? {
~ x = 2 and x = -2 #Those solve x squared minus 4 equals 0.
= x = 2i and x = -2i #Correct. x squared equals -4, so x equals plus or minus 2i.
~ x = 4i and x = -4i #This uses 4 instead of the square root of 4.
~ x = -2 only #There are two complex solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q12::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q12<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> + 36 = 0? {
~ x = 6 and x = -6 #These solve x squared minus 36 equals 0.
~ x = 36i and x = -36i #This uses the radicand instead of its square root.
= x = 6i and x = -6i #Correct. x squared equals -36, so x equals plus or minus 6i.
~ x = -36 and x = 36 #These are real values and do not solve the equation.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q13::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q13<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> - 8x + 20 = 0? {
~ x = 4 and x = -4 #These do not satisfy the equation.
= x = 4 + 2i and x = 4 - 2i #Correct. The quadratic formula gives (8 plus or minus &radic;-16) divided by 2, which is 4 plus or minus 2i.
~ x = -4 + 2i and x = -4 - 2i #The real part should be positive 4.
~ x = 8 + 2i and x = 8 - 2i #This does not divide the numerator by 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q14::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q14<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 10 = 0? {
~ x = 1 + 3i and x = 1 - 3i #The real part should be -1 because -b divided by 2a is -1.
= x = -1 + 3i and x = -1 - 3i #Correct. The discriminant is -36, so the solutions are -1 plus or minus 3i.
~ x = -2 + 3i and x = -2 - 3i #This does not divide -b by 2a correctly.
~ x = -1 + 6i and x = -1 - 6i #This does not divide the square-root part by 2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q15::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q15<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> - 12x + 40 = 0? {
~ x = 6 + 4i and x = 6 - 4i #The imaginary part should be 2i after simplifying and dividing by 2.
~ x = -6 + 2i and x = -6 - 2i #The real part should be positive 6.
= x = 6 + 2i and x = 6 - 2i #Correct. The discriminant is -16, so the solutions are 6 plus or minus 2i.
~ x = 12 + 2i and x = 12 - 2i #This does not divide -b by 2a.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q16::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q16<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of 2x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 10 = 0? {
~ x = 1 + 4i and x = 1 - 4i #The imaginary part is too large because the square-root part must be divided by 4.
= x = 1 + 2i and x = 1 - 2i #Correct. The quadratic formula gives (4 plus or minus &radic;-64) divided by 4, which is 1 plus or minus 2i.
~ x = -1 + 2i and x = -1 - 2i #The real part should be positive 1.
~ x = 2 + 2i and x = 2 - 2i #This does not divide the real part by 4 correctly.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q17::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q17<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of 3x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 12 = 0? {
= x = -1 + i&radic;3 and x = -1 - i&radic;3 #Correct. Dividing by 3 gives x squared plus 2x plus 4 equals 0, which has solutions -1 plus or minus i radical 3.
~ x = 1 + i&radic;3 and x = 1 - i&radic;3 #The real part should be negative.
~ x = -1 + 3i and x = -1 - 3i #The square root simplifies to i radical 3, not 3i.
~ x = -2 + i&radic;3 and x = -2 - i&radic;3 #The real part is not -2.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q18::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q18<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has complex non-real solutions? {
~ x<sup>2</sup> - 9 = 0 #This has real solutions 3 and -3.
= x<sup>2</sup> + 9 = 0 #Correct. This equation requires x squared to equal -9, so the solutions are complex.
~ x<sup>2</sup> - 6x + 9 = 0 #This has one repeated real solution.
~ x<sup>2</sup> - 4x + 3 = 0 #This has two real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q19::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q19<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has one repeated real solution? {
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 16 = 0 #This has complex non-real solutions.
~ x<sup>2</sup> - 5x + 4 = 0 #This has two different real solutions.
= x<sup>2</sup> - 10x + 25 = 0 #Correct. This is (x - 5) squared equals 0, so it has one repeated real solution.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 2x + 5 = 0 #This has a negative discriminant and complex solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q20::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q20<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which equation has two real solutions? {
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 4x + 8 = 0 #The discriminant is negative, so the solutions are complex.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 6x + 9 = 0 #This has one repeated real solution.
~ x<sup>2</sup> + 25 = 0 #This has complex non-real solutions.
= x<sup>2</sup> - x - 6 = 0 #Correct. This factors as (x - 3)(x + 2), giving two real solutions.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q21::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q21<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>If one solution of a quadratic equation with real coefficients is 5 + 3i, what is the other solution? {
~ -5 - 3i #The real part should not change sign.
= 5 - 3i #Correct. Complex non-real solutions of quadratics with real coefficients occur in conjugate pairs.
~ -5 + 3i #This changes the real part instead of the imaginary sign.
~ 3 + 5i #This switches the real and imaginary parts.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q22::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q22<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>If one solution of a quadratic equation with real coefficients is -2 - 7i, what is the other solution? {
= -2 + 7i #Correct. The conjugate keeps the real part and changes the sign of the imaginary part.
~ 2 + 7i #This changes the real part, which should stay -2.
~ -7 - 2i #This switches the parts of the complex number.
~ 2 - 7i #This changes the real part but not the imaginary sign.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q23::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q23<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which expression correctly rewrites &radic;-80? {
~ 4&radic;5 #This omits i, so it is real instead of imaginary.
~ 80i #This does not simplify the square root.
= 4i&radic;5 #Correct. &radic;-80 = i&radic;80 = 4i&radic;5.
~ 8i&radic;10 #This does not equal the simplified square root of 80.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q24::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q24<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>What are the solutions of x<sup>2</sup> + 14x + 53 = 0? {
= x = -7 + 2i and x = -7 - 2i #Correct. The discriminant is -16, so the solutions are -7 plus or minus 2i.
~ x = 7 + 2i and x = 7 - 2i #The real part should be -7 because -b divided by 2a is -7.
~ x = -14 + 2i and x = -14 - 2i #This does not divide -b by 2a.
~ x = -7 + 4i and x = -7 - 4i #The square-root part must be divided by 2, giving 2i.
}

::ALG2_U01_L04_Q25::
Question ID:<br>ALG2_U01_L04_Q25<br><br>MLA Standard:<br>MLA.A2.PF.1<br><br>Question:<br>Which explanation correctly describes why x<sup>2</sup> + 1 = 0 has complex solutions? {
~ The equation factors as (x + 1)(x - 1), so the solutions are real. #That factorization equals x squared minus 1, not x squared plus 1.
= The equation gives x<sup>2</sup> = -1, so x = i and x = -i. #Correct. The equation requires square roots of a negative number.
~ The discriminant is positive, so there are two complex solutions. #A positive discriminant gives real solutions, not complex non-real solutions.
~ The equation has no solutions in the complex number system. #The complex number system includes i and -i as solutions.
}
